1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,389
We remember the nineteen fifties as an

2
00:00:02,389 --> 00:00:03,018
easy time.

3
00:00:03,198 --> 00:00:04,802
And in some ways,

4
00:00:04,802 --> 00:00:05,462
it was.

5
00:00:05,642 --> 00:00:07,769
But ask the people who actually ran

6
00:00:07,769 --> 00:00:09,106
a household back then,

7
00:00:09,106 --> 00:00:11,598
and they'll tell you something different.

8
00:00:11,778 --> 00:00:14,199
Daily life in the fifties took a

9
00:00:14,199 --> 00:00:15,107
lot of hard,

10
00:00:15,107 --> 00:00:16,167
physical work.

11
00:00:16,347 --> 00:00:19,038
Things we don't even think about today

12
00:00:19,038 --> 00:00:21,234
took hours, sweat and patience.

13
00:00:21,584 --> 00:00:23,384
This isn't a sad story.

14
00:00:23,564 --> 00:00:25,770
It's a story about how tough and

15
00:00:25,770 --> 00:00:28,045
resourceful ordinary people were.

16
00:00:28,225 --> 00:00:30,331
The things they did every day,

17
00:00:30,331 --> 00:00:33,279
without complaining, would wear most of us

18
00:00:33,279 --> 00:00:34,472
out by lunchtime.

19
00:00:34,652 --> 00:00:37,837
So let's follow one ordinary family through

20
00:00:37,837 --> 00:00:40,875
one ordinary day in nineteen fifty-three,

21
00:00:40,875 --> 00:00:43,394
from before sunrise until bedtime.

22
00:00:43,743 --> 00:00:44,332
As we go,

23
00:00:44,332 --> 00:00:46,491
you might start to remember a few

24
00:00:46,491 --> 00:00:49,435
things your own parents and grandparents did,

25
00:00:49,435 --> 00:00:51,267
that you'd almost forgotten.

26
00:00:51,447 --> 00:00:53,509
And you might appreciate them a little

27
00:00:53,509 --> 00:00:53,780
more.

28
00:00:54,130 --> 00:00:55,676
The day starts early,

29
00:00:55,676 --> 00:00:56,707
and in winter,

30
00:00:56,707 --> 00:00:57,811
it starts cold.

31
00:00:57,991 --> 00:00:59,299
In nineteen fifty,

32
00:00:59,299 --> 00:01:01,915
about a third of American homes were

33
00:01:01,915 --> 00:01:03,587
still heated with coal.

34
00:01:03,767 --> 00:01:05,650
Before anyone else woke up,

35
00:01:05,650 --> 00:01:07,883
Dad went down to the basement to

36
00:01:07,883 --> 00:01:10,324
shake down the ashes in the furnace

37
00:01:10,324 --> 00:01:12,068
and shovel in fresh coal.

38
00:01:12,248 --> 00:01:13,690
Until the fire caught,

39
00:01:13,690 --> 00:01:15,787
you could see your breath in the

40
00:01:15,787 --> 00:01:16,311
bedroom.

41
00:01:16,661 --> 00:01:18,612
Coal was delivered by truck,

42
00:01:18,612 --> 00:01:20,702
and poured down a chute into a

43
00:01:20,702 --> 00:01:22,443
coal bin in the basement.

44
00:01:22,623 --> 00:01:24,957
It left a black dust on everything.

45
00:01:25,137 --> 00:01:27,176
The ashes had to be carried out

46
00:01:27,176 --> 00:01:27,900
in buckets.

47
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:29,598
And in many farmhouses,

48
00:01:29,598 --> 00:01:31,446
there was no furnace at all,

49
00:01:31,446 --> 00:01:33,558
just a wood stove in the kitchen

50
00:01:33,558 --> 00:01:36,000
that somebody had to keep burning all

51
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:36,462
winter.

52
00:01:36,813 --> 00:01:37,723
In the summer,

53
00:01:37,723 --> 00:01:39,611
the problem was the opposite.

54
00:01:39,791 --> 00:01:42,078
Air conditioning in homes was rare.

55
00:01:42,258 --> 00:01:44,040
Even by nineteen sixty,

56
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:46,986
only about one in eight American homes

57
00:01:46,986 --> 00:01:47,529
had it.

58
00:01:47,709 --> 00:01:48,724
On hot nights,

59
00:01:48,724 --> 00:01:51,407
families slept with the windows open,

60
00:01:51,407 --> 00:01:53,728
moved mattresses onto the porch,

61
00:01:53,728 --> 00:01:55,469
or lay in front of a box

62
00:01:55,469 --> 00:01:57,862
fan with a wet washcloth on their

63
00:01:57,862 --> 00:01:58,587
foreheads.

64
00:01:58,938 --> 00:02:00,949
Then came the morning rush,

65
00:02:00,949 --> 00:02:03,779
and most houses had just one bathroom.

66
00:02:03,959 --> 00:02:06,088
A family of six had to share

67
00:02:06,088 --> 00:02:06,316
it.

68
00:02:06,496 --> 00:02:07,837
Dad shaved first,

69
00:02:07,837 --> 00:02:10,362
because he had to catch the bus.

70
00:02:10,542 --> 00:02:11,859
Then the teenagers,

71
00:02:11,859 --> 00:02:13,038
who took forever.

72
00:02:13,218 --> 00:02:14,379
Then the little ones.

73
00:02:14,559 --> 00:02:17,571
Somebody was always pounding on the door,

74
00:02:17,571 --> 00:02:19,702
and somebody was always late.

75
00:02:20,053 --> 00:02:22,574
Many older houses had no shower,

76
00:02:22,574 --> 00:02:23,756
just a bathtub.

77
00:02:23,936 --> 00:02:26,039
Hot water came from a small tank

78
00:02:26,039 --> 00:02:27,419
that ran out quickly.

79
00:02:27,599 --> 00:02:30,221
So baths were often taken once or

80
00:02:30,221 --> 00:02:31,253
twice a week,

81
00:02:31,253 --> 00:02:32,683
on Saturday night,

82
00:02:32,683 --> 00:02:36,179
and sometimes children shared the bathwater,

83
00:02:36,179 --> 00:02:37,688
youngest to oldest.

84
00:02:37,868 --> 00:02:40,668
In between, people washed at the sink

85
00:02:40,668 --> 00:02:41,955
with a washcloth.

86
00:02:42,305 --> 00:02:44,609
And remember, in nineteen fifty,

87
00:02:44,609 --> 00:02:47,130
more than a third of American homes

88
00:02:47,130 --> 00:02:49,363
still lacked complete plumbing.

89
00:02:49,543 --> 00:02:51,035
In the countryside,

90
00:02:51,035 --> 00:02:53,939
many families still used an outhouse,

91
00:02:53,939 --> 00:02:55,116
even in winter.

92
00:02:55,296 --> 00:02:57,680
A chamber pot under the bed saved

93
00:02:57,680 --> 00:02:59,774
a cold trip outside at night,

94
00:02:59,774 --> 00:03:02,230
and somebody had to empty it every

95
00:03:02,230 --> 00:03:02,808
morning.

96
00:03:03,158 --> 00:03:05,131
Breakfast was made from scratch.

97
00:03:05,311 --> 00:03:08,241
Eggs fried in bacon grease saved from

98
00:03:08,241 --> 00:03:10,617
yesterday, in a cast iron pan.

99
00:03:10,797 --> 00:03:12,617
Toast made under the broiler,

100
00:03:12,617 --> 00:03:14,562
or in a toaster that didn't pop

101
00:03:14,562 --> 00:03:15,441
up on its own.

102
00:03:15,621 --> 00:03:18,013
Coffee boiled in a percolator.

103
00:03:18,193 --> 00:03:20,027
Oatmeal stirred on the stove.

104
00:03:20,207 --> 00:03:22,209
There were very few shortcuts,

105
00:03:22,209 --> 00:03:24,477
and everything had to be washed by

106
00:03:24,477 --> 00:03:25,478
hand afterward.

107
00:03:25,828 --> 00:03:28,776
Most kitchens had a refrigerator by then,

108
00:03:28,776 --> 00:03:31,077
but many had only a tiny freezer

109
00:03:31,077 --> 00:03:33,953
compartment, just big enough for two ice

110
00:03:33,953 --> 00:03:34,744
cube trays.

111
00:03:34,924 --> 00:03:35,864
It frosted up,

112
00:03:35,864 --> 00:03:37,812
and every few weeks it had to

113
00:03:37,812 --> 00:03:39,223
be defrosted by hand,

114
00:03:39,223 --> 00:03:41,104
with pans of hot water and a

115
00:03:41,104 --> 00:03:43,388
butter knife to chip away the ice.

116
00:03:43,568 --> 00:03:46,905
Some older families still called it the

117
00:03:46,905 --> 00:03:47,503
icebox.

118
00:03:47,854 --> 00:03:50,547
Lunches were packed in paper bags or

119
00:03:50,547 --> 00:03:51,894
metal lunch boxes.

120
00:03:52,074 --> 00:03:54,674
Sandwiches wrapped in wax paper,

121
00:03:54,674 --> 00:03:57,763
because plastic wrap was still new and

122
00:03:57,763 --> 00:03:58,575
expensive.

123
00:03:58,755 --> 00:04:01,135
A thermos of milk or soup.

124
00:04:01,315 --> 00:04:01,919
An apple.

125
00:04:02,099 --> 00:04:04,967
Every morning, a mother might make five

126
00:04:04,967 --> 00:04:07,614
or six lunches before eight o'clock.

127
00:04:07,964 --> 00:04:10,077
Getting to work wasn't easy.

128
00:04:10,257 --> 00:04:12,900
Many families still had only one car,

129
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:13,972
or none at all.

130
00:04:14,152 --> 00:04:15,585
In nineteen fifty,

131
00:04:15,585 --> 00:04:18,690
about four in ten households didn't own

132
00:04:18,690 --> 00:04:19,167
a car.

133
00:04:19,347 --> 00:04:21,064
Fathers took the bus,

134
00:04:21,064 --> 00:04:23,271
the streetcar or the train.

135
00:04:23,451 --> 00:04:25,088
Some walked miles.

136
00:04:25,268 --> 00:04:27,598
Some rode with a neighbor who worked

137
00:04:27,598 --> 00:04:28,763
at the same plant.

138
00:04:29,113 --> 00:04:31,713
And the cars themselves were less reliable.

139
00:04:31,893 --> 00:04:34,076
No power steering in most of them.

140
00:04:34,256 --> 00:04:35,835
No power brakes.

141
00:04:36,015 --> 00:04:37,904
In winter, they wouldn't start,

142
00:04:37,904 --> 00:04:40,038
and men pushed them down the street

143
00:04:40,038 --> 00:04:41,135
to get them going.

144
00:04:41,315 --> 00:04:43,048
Tires went flat often,

145
00:04:43,048 --> 00:04:45,806
and every driver knew how to change

146
00:04:45,806 --> 00:04:46,121
one.

147
00:04:46,301 --> 00:04:48,712
A car with a hundred thousand miles

148
00:04:48,712 --> 00:04:50,365
was considered worn out.

149
00:04:50,715 --> 00:04:52,265
The work week was long,

150
00:04:52,265 --> 00:04:54,557
and much of the work was physical.

151
00:04:54,737 --> 00:04:56,962
Steel mills, coal mines,

152
00:04:56,962 --> 00:05:00,670
factories, farms and construction sites.

153
00:05:00,850 --> 00:05:02,697
In nineteen fifty-three,

154
00:05:02,697 --> 00:05:07,312
workplace accidents killed around fifteen thousand Americans

155
00:05:07,312 --> 00:05:07,851
a year.

156
00:05:08,031 --> 00:05:10,532
Many men came home with injuries that

157
00:05:10,532 --> 00:05:11,816
never fully healed.

158
00:05:12,166 --> 00:05:13,074
Back at home,

159
00:05:13,074 --> 00:05:14,401
Monday was washday,

160
00:05:14,401 --> 00:05:16,217
and it took the whole day.

161
00:05:16,397 --> 00:05:19,486
Many families still used a wringer washer.

162
00:05:19,666 --> 00:05:21,881
You filled the tub with a hose

163
00:05:21,881 --> 00:05:23,654
or buckets of hot water,

164
00:05:23,654 --> 00:05:25,796
added soap, and let it churn.

165
00:05:25,976 --> 00:05:28,129
Then you fed each piece of clothing

166
00:05:28,129 --> 00:05:30,467
by hand through two rollers to squeeze

167
00:05:30,467 --> 00:05:31,328
out the water.

168
00:05:31,508 --> 00:05:33,702
Fingers sometimes got caught.

169
00:05:34,053 --> 00:05:36,936
Then everything went outside to dry on

170
00:05:36,936 --> 00:05:40,426
the clothesline, held with wooden clothespins.

171
00:05:40,606 --> 00:05:43,305
In winter, clothes froze stiff on the

172
00:05:43,305 --> 00:05:45,784
line, and came inside like boards.

173
00:05:45,964 --> 00:05:46,927
If it rained,

174
00:05:46,927 --> 00:05:49,593
everything came in fast and was hung

175
00:05:49,593 --> 00:05:51,444
on lines in the basement.

176
00:05:51,624 --> 00:05:53,718
Electric dryers existed,

177
00:05:53,718 --> 00:05:55,375
but in fifty-three,

178
00:05:55,375 --> 00:05:58,079
only a small share of homes had

179
00:05:58,079 --> 00:05:58,428
one.

180
00:05:58,778 --> 00:06:00,891
And Tuesday was ironing day.

181
00:06:01,071 --> 00:06:03,440
Nearly everything was cotton or wool,

182
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:05,424
and nearly everything wrinkled.

183
00:06:05,604 --> 00:06:07,921
Shirts, dresses, sheets,

184
00:06:07,921 --> 00:06:10,817
tablecloths and handkerchiefs.

185
00:06:10,997 --> 00:06:13,261
Women sprinkled them with water,

186
00:06:13,261 --> 00:06:14,323
rolled them up,

187
00:06:14,323 --> 00:06:16,657
and ironed for hours with a heavy

188
00:06:16,657 --> 00:06:17,011
iron.

189
00:06:17,191 --> 00:06:19,855
Many housewives did more than ten hours

190
00:06:19,855 --> 00:06:21,905
of laundry and ironing a week.

191
00:06:22,255 --> 00:06:24,066
Shopping took time too.

192
00:06:24,246 --> 00:06:26,256
The supermarket was growing,

193
00:06:26,256 --> 00:06:28,840
but many women still went to several

194
00:06:28,840 --> 00:06:29,342
stores.

195
00:06:29,522 --> 00:06:31,025
The butcher for meat,

196
00:06:31,025 --> 00:06:32,528
the bakery for bread,

197
00:06:32,528 --> 00:06:35,246
the corner grocery for milk and canned

198
00:06:35,246 --> 00:06:35,676
goods.

199
00:06:35,856 --> 00:06:37,929
Many walked or took the bus,

200
00:06:37,929 --> 00:06:39,780
carrying heavy bags home.

201
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:41,998
Food was bought more often,

202
00:06:41,998 --> 00:06:44,488
because refrigerators were small.

203
00:06:44,838 --> 00:06:47,496
And money was tight in many homes.

204
00:06:47,676 --> 00:06:51,411
Women kept careful budgets in little notebooks,

205
00:06:51,411 --> 00:06:54,509
and some kept cash in envelopes labeled

206
00:06:54,509 --> 00:06:57,847
for rent, groceries and the electric bill.

207
00:06:58,027 --> 00:06:59,340
Meat was expensive,

208
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:02,174
so cheaper cuts were stretched with bread

209
00:07:02,174 --> 00:07:03,971
crumbs, rice and potatoes.

210
00:07:04,151 --> 00:07:05,579
Nothing was wasted.

211
00:07:05,759 --> 00:07:07,547
Leftovers became soup.

212
00:07:07,897 --> 00:07:09,034
In the afternoon,

213
00:07:09,034 --> 00:07:11,109
a mother might need to call the

214
00:07:11,109 --> 00:07:11,577
doctor.

215
00:07:11,757 --> 00:07:12,979
But many families,

216
00:07:12,979 --> 00:07:14,743
especially in the country,

217
00:07:14,743 --> 00:07:16,169
were on a party line.

218
00:07:16,349 --> 00:07:19,065
That meant several households shared the same

219
00:07:19,065 --> 00:07:19,971
telephone line.

220
00:07:20,151 --> 00:07:22,485
Each house had its own ring pattern.

221
00:07:22,665 --> 00:07:24,550
Two shorts and a long,

222
00:07:24,550 --> 00:07:25,579
for example.

223
00:07:25,759 --> 00:07:28,004
And anyone could pick up and listen

224
00:07:28,004 --> 00:07:28,197
in.

225
00:07:28,547 --> 00:07:30,938
Long distance calls were expensive,

226
00:07:30,938 --> 00:07:33,876
and families saved them for emergencies and

227
00:07:33,876 --> 00:07:34,491
holidays.

228
00:07:34,671 --> 00:07:37,006
A call across the country could cost

229
00:07:37,006 --> 00:07:39,211
several dollars for a few minutes,

230
00:07:39,211 --> 00:07:40,898
at a time when a man might

231
00:07:40,898 --> 00:07:42,519
earn two dollars an hour.

232
00:07:42,699 --> 00:07:44,963
When the phone rang long distance,

233
00:07:44,963 --> 00:07:47,293
everyone in the house came running,

234
00:07:47,293 --> 00:07:49,224
because it might be bad news.

235
00:07:49,574 --> 00:07:51,783
Getting sick was more dangerous,

236
00:07:51,783 --> 00:07:53,440
especially for children.

237
00:07:53,620 --> 00:07:57,021
Measles infected millions of American children every

238
00:07:57,021 --> 00:07:59,703
year, and killed several hundred of them.

239
00:07:59,883 --> 00:08:01,623
Whooping cough, mumps,

240
00:08:01,623 --> 00:08:05,499
chickenpox and scarlet fever went through schools

241
00:08:05,499 --> 00:08:06,211
in waves.

242
00:08:06,391 --> 00:08:09,777
Houses with a contagious child sometimes had

243
00:08:09,777 --> 00:08:12,624
a quarantine sign posted on the front

244
00:08:12,624 --> 00:08:13,008
door.

245
00:08:13,359 --> 00:08:16,936
Antibiotics like penicillin were still fairly new,

246
00:08:16,936 --> 00:08:19,512
and they were seen as miracle drugs.

247
00:08:19,692 --> 00:08:22,206
But many other medicines we take for

248
00:08:22,206 --> 00:08:23,953
granted didn't exist yet.

249
00:08:24,133 --> 00:08:25,926
Medical care was simpler,

250
00:08:25,926 --> 00:08:28,579
and families relied on home remedies.

251
00:08:28,759 --> 00:08:31,397
Mustard plasters, castor oil,

252
00:08:31,397 --> 00:08:34,216
Vicks VapoRub and chicken soup.

253
00:08:34,566 --> 00:08:36,684
And the dentist was something to be

254
00:08:36,684 --> 00:08:37,108
feared.

255
00:08:37,288 --> 00:08:40,647
Fluoride in drinking water was just beginning

256
00:08:40,647 --> 00:08:42,513
to spread in the fifties.

257
00:08:42,693 --> 00:08:45,641
Many adults had lost several teeth by

258
00:08:45,641 --> 00:08:48,670
middle age, and plenty had dentures by

259
00:08:48,670 --> 00:08:49,148
fifty.

260
00:08:49,328 --> 00:08:51,265
Drills were slow and loud,

261
00:08:51,265 --> 00:08:54,097
and the dentist didn't always use much

262
00:08:54,097 --> 00:08:54,842
novocaine.

263
00:08:55,193 --> 00:08:56,210
By late afternoon,

264
00:08:56,210 --> 00:08:57,962
dinner had to be ready when Dad

265
00:08:57,962 --> 00:08:59,035
walked in the door.

266
00:08:59,215 --> 00:09:01,661
Everything was cooked on the stove or

267
00:09:01,661 --> 00:09:02,455
in the oven.

268
00:09:02,635 --> 00:09:05,534
No microwave, no dishwasher,

269
00:09:05,534 --> 00:09:07,813
very few frozen meals.

270
00:09:07,993 --> 00:09:10,373
A pot roast took three hours.

271
00:09:10,553 --> 00:09:13,002
Potatoes had to be peeled by hand.

272
00:09:13,182 --> 00:09:15,446
Pies were made with homemade crust.

273
00:09:15,797 --> 00:09:17,770
After dinner came the dishes.

274
00:09:17,950 --> 00:09:19,698
In nineteen fifty-three,

275
00:09:19,698 --> 00:09:22,100
only a tiny fraction of homes had

276
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:23,047
a dishwasher.

277
00:09:23,227 --> 00:09:25,311
So the dishes were washed in the

278
00:09:25,311 --> 00:09:26,222
sink, by hand.

279
00:09:26,402 --> 00:09:28,904
Kids took turns washing and drying,

280
00:09:28,904 --> 00:09:31,406
and argued about whose turn it was.

281
00:09:31,586 --> 00:09:33,854
A big family dinner could mean an

282
00:09:33,854 --> 00:09:35,023
hour at the sink.

283
00:09:35,373 --> 00:09:36,287
In the evening,

284
00:09:36,287 --> 00:09:39,030
many families gathered around the television.

285
00:09:39,210 --> 00:09:40,511
But in fifty-three,

286
00:09:40,511 --> 00:09:42,291
that television was small,

287
00:09:42,291 --> 00:09:45,373
with a round-cornered black and white screen,

288
00:09:45,373 --> 00:09:47,291
maybe two or three channels,

289
00:09:47,291 --> 00:09:50,030
and a picture that rolled and flickered.

290
00:09:50,210 --> 00:09:52,298
Someone had to get up and adjust

291
00:09:52,298 --> 00:09:53,799
the rabbit-ear antenna,

292
00:09:53,799 --> 00:09:55,627
or climb on the roof to turn

293
00:09:55,627 --> 00:09:56,410
the big one.

294
00:09:56,759 --> 00:09:58,689
Television tubes burned out,

295
00:09:58,689 --> 00:10:00,549
and you had to take them to

296
00:10:00,549 --> 00:10:02,685
the drugstore to test them on a

297
00:10:02,685 --> 00:10:04,132
tube-testing machine.

298
00:10:04,312 --> 00:10:05,844
Radios did the same.

299
00:10:06,024 --> 00:10:08,044
Repairmen made house calls.

300
00:10:08,224 --> 00:10:10,628
And many families didn't have a TV

301
00:10:10,628 --> 00:10:11,406
at all yet.

302
00:10:11,586 --> 00:10:13,743
They listened to the radio,

303
00:10:13,743 --> 00:10:16,700
read, sewed, played cards or just sat

304
00:10:16,700 --> 00:10:17,739
on the porch.

305
00:10:18,088 --> 00:10:20,712
Mothers often kept working after dinner.

306
00:10:20,892 --> 00:10:22,773
Mending socks with a darning egg.

307
00:10:22,953 --> 00:10:25,252
Letting out hems as the children grew.

308
00:10:25,432 --> 00:10:27,603
Sewing a new dress from a pattern.

309
00:10:27,783 --> 00:10:29,768
Making a Halloween costume.

310
00:10:29,948 --> 00:10:32,391
Many women sewed most of their family's

311
00:10:32,391 --> 00:10:35,022
clothes, and their hands were never still.

312
00:10:35,371 --> 00:10:37,347
And then, finally,

313
00:10:37,347 --> 00:10:37,786
bed.

314
00:10:37,966 --> 00:10:40,265
Many children shared a bed with a

315
00:10:40,265 --> 00:10:41,519
brother or sister.

316
00:10:41,699 --> 00:10:43,117
Houses were small,

317
00:10:43,117 --> 00:10:45,874
often under a thousand square feet,

318
00:10:45,874 --> 00:10:48,395
with two or three bedrooms for a

319
00:10:48,395 --> 00:10:50,128
family of five or six.

320
00:10:50,308 --> 00:10:52,812
The quiet of the night was broken

321
00:10:52,812 --> 00:10:54,253
by a train whistle,

322
00:10:54,253 --> 00:10:55,316
a dog barking,

323
00:10:55,316 --> 00:10:57,440
or a baby crying in the next

324
00:10:57,440 --> 00:10:57,819
room.

325
00:10:58,169 --> 00:10:59,129
Add it all up,

326
00:10:59,129 --> 00:11:00,294
and it was a lot.

327
00:11:00,474 --> 00:11:03,376
Studies from the time found that full-time

328
00:11:03,376 --> 00:11:06,348
housewives worked around fifty hours a week

329
00:11:06,348 --> 00:11:07,661
on housework alone.

330
00:11:07,841 --> 00:11:09,156
Men worked long,

331
00:11:09,156 --> 00:11:12,937
hard shifts, often with overtime on Saturdays.

332
00:11:13,117 --> 00:11:16,229
Children had chores before and after school.

333
00:11:16,409 --> 00:11:18,708
Almost nobody had what we would call

334
00:11:18,708 --> 00:11:19,346
free time.

335
00:11:19,696 --> 00:11:22,369
So why do people remember the fifties

336
00:11:22,369 --> 00:11:22,947
as easy?

337
00:11:23,127 --> 00:11:26,524
Partly because, compared with the Depression and

338
00:11:26,524 --> 00:11:28,294
the war that came before,

339
00:11:28,294 --> 00:11:29,780
it really was easier.

340
00:11:29,960 --> 00:11:31,530
People had steady jobs,

341
00:11:31,530 --> 00:11:33,988
enough food and hope for the future.

342
00:11:34,168 --> 00:11:36,855
For people who had lived through hunger

343
00:11:36,855 --> 00:11:38,783
and fear, a cold house and a

344
00:11:38,783 --> 00:11:41,332
wringer washer felt like a good life.

345
00:11:41,681 --> 00:11:44,227
And partly because the hard work brought

346
00:11:44,227 --> 00:11:45,246
people together.

347
00:11:45,426 --> 00:11:47,957
Families worked side by side.

348
00:11:48,137 --> 00:11:49,762
Neighbors helped each other.

349
00:11:49,942 --> 00:11:51,707
Children learned to be useful.

350
00:11:51,887 --> 00:11:54,352
When people remember the fifties fondly,

351
00:11:54,352 --> 00:11:56,879
they're not remembering the coal furnace.

352
00:11:57,059 --> 00:11:59,366
They're remembering the people who stood next

353
00:11:59,366 --> 00:12:01,006
to them while they did the work.

354
00:12:01,356 --> 00:12:04,097
If your parents or grandparents lived through

355
00:12:04,097 --> 00:12:06,168
those years, now you know a little

356
00:12:06,168 --> 00:12:08,482
more about what their days were really

357
00:12:08,482 --> 00:12:08,787
like.

358
00:12:08,967 --> 00:12:09,904
They were tough,

359
00:12:09,904 --> 00:12:11,486
and they rarely complained.

360
00:12:11,666 --> 00:12:13,581
Next time you push a button and

361
00:12:13,581 --> 00:12:14,816
the laundry is done,

362
00:12:14,816 --> 00:12:16,299
you might think of them.

363
00:12:16,648 --> 00:12:18,564
So tell us in the comments.

364
00:12:18,744 --> 00:12:20,755
What was the hardest chore you remember

365
00:12:20,755 --> 00:12:21,786
from your childhood?

366
00:12:21,966 --> 00:12:24,413
Did your family have a coal furnace,

367
00:12:24,413 --> 00:12:26,656
a wringer washer or a party line?

368
00:12:26,836 --> 00:12:28,427
We read every comment.

369
00:12:28,607 --> 00:12:30,938
Next, we're looking at what it cost

370
00:12:30,938 --> 00:12:33,270
to buy your first house in nineteen

371
00:12:33,270 --> 00:12:33,936
fifty-six.

372
00:12:34,116 --> 00:12:35,942
Thank you for spending this time with

373
00:12:35,942 --> 00:12:36,090
me.
